​This isn’t a typical hackathon—it's a collaboration for builders, developers, adopters, and anyone interested in rapidly building and iterating on AI agents.

​A Personal Agent Needs a Personal Brain:

​In this hands-on hackathon, participants will build a Personal Brain — an AI-powered memory system that can organize and connect information from your notes, documents, conversations, projects, links, and other personal knowledge.

​Instead of starting every conversation with an AI assistant from scratch, your Personal Brain gives agents access to persistent context: what you're working on, what you've learned, the people and projects that matter to you, and the connections between them.

​During the hackathon, you'll choose a personal use case, add your own data, build a searchable and connected memory, and then create an AI agent or application that can use that memory to answer questions and help you get things done.

​Possible projects include:

​A networking brain that remembers people, companies, meetings, and relationships
​A personal knowledge assistant that writes blog posts
​A career brain that understands your experience, projects, and accomplishments
​A research assistant that uses articles you give it to perform deep research
​A project brain that remembers decisions, tasks, and conversations and helps you manage a project
​A life assistant that can recall important information across months or years
​The challenge: In just 3 hours, build a personal agent that doesn't just have intelligence—it has your memory and context.

​Anyone who wants feedback during the hackathon will get a chance.

​By the end of the hackathon, every participant should have their own personal brain and an agent that can put that memory to work.